:"\u7F51\u7EDC\u5F02\u5E38\uFF0C\u8BF7\u7A0D\u540E\u91CD\u8BD5","toast_processing":"\u6B63\u5728\u5904\u7406...","toast_saving":"\u6B63\u5728\u4FDD\u5B58...","toast_savingFailed":"\u4FDD\u5B58\u5931\u8D25\uFF0C\u8BF7\u68C0\u67E5\u8F93\u5165","toast_skill_image_lengthlimit":"\u8F93\u5165\u5185\u5BB9\u8FC7\u957F","toast_upload_fail_image":"\u56FE\u7247\u4E0A\u4F20\u5931\u8D25","too_many_wrong_attempts_try_agagain_toast":"\u8BF710\u5206\u949F\u540E\u91CD\u8BD5","tool_get_currenttime_complete_cn":"\u5DF2\u83B7\u53D6\u7CBE\u786E\u65F6\u95F4","tool_get_currenttime_doing_cn":"\u6B63\u5728\u83B7\u53D6\u7CBE\u786E\u65F6\u95F4","tool_groups.bash":"Executes a given bash command and returns its output.\\n\\nThe working directory persists between commands, but shell state does not. Environment variables, aliases, functions, and shell options set in one Bash call do NOT carry over to the next call. Use absolute paths instead of relying on variables / `cd` aliases set in a prior turn.\\n\\nIMPORTANT: Avoid using this tool to run `find`, `grep`, `cat`, `head`, `tail`, `sed`, `awk`, or `echo` commands, unless explicitly instructed. Instead, use the appropriate dedicated tool:\\n - File search: Use Glob (NOT find or ls)\\n - Content search: Use Grep (NOT grep or rg)\\n - Read files: Use Read (NOT cat/head/tail)\\n - Edit files: Use Edit (NOT sed/awk)\\n - Write files: Use Write (NOT echo >/cat <<EOF)\\n\\n### Instructions\\n - If your command will create new directories or files, first run `ls` to verify the parent directory exists.\\n - Always quote file paths that contain spaces with double quotes.\\n - You may specify an optional timeout in milliseconds (up to 600000ms / 10 minutes). Default timeout is 120000ms (2 minutes).\\n - Use `run_in_background: true` for long-running commands. This is the only way to obtain a tracked `backgroundTaskId` you can pass to TaskOutput / TaskStop. Manually appending `&` runs the command in the V25 session (no SIGHUP), but you lose the task id and cannot reliably wait on it.\\n - Foreground commands that exceed ~15 seconds are automatically moved to the background and you\'\'ll receive a `backgroundTaskId`; use `TaskOutput(task_id, block=true, timeout=<ms>)` to wait for completion.\\n - When issuing multiple commands: use \'\'&&\'\' to chain dependent commands, or make multiple Bash tool calls for independent commands.","tool_groups.edit":"Performs exact string replacements in files.\\n\\nUsage:\\n- You must use your Read tool at least once before editing.\\n- Preserve the exact indentation (tabs/spaces) from the file.\\n- ALWAYS prefer editing existing files. NEVER write new files unless explicitly required.\\n- The edit will FAIL if `old_string` is not unique in the file. Provide more surrounding context to make it unique, or use `replace_all`.\\n- Use `replace_all` for renaming variables across the file.","tool_groups.glob":"Fast file pattern matching tool that works with any codebase size.\\n- Supports glob patterns like \'\'**/*.js\'\' or \'\'src/**/*.ts\'\'\\n- Returns matching file paths sorted by modification time\\n- Use this tool when you need to find files by name patterns\\n- You can call multiple Glob tools in a single response for batch searching.","tool_groups.grep":"A powerful search tool built on ripgrep.\\n\\nUsage:\\n- ALWAYS use Grep for search tasks. NEVER invoke `grep` or `rg` as a Bash command.\\n- Supports full regex syntax (e.g., \'\'log.*Error\'\', \'\'function\\\\s+\\\\w+\'\')\\n- Filter files with glob parameter (e.g., \'\'*.js\'\') or type parameter (e.g., \'\'py\'\')\\n- Output modes: \'\'content\'\' shows matching lines, \'\'files_with_matches\'\' shows file paths (default), \'\'count\'\' shows match counts\\n- Multiline matching: use `multiline: true` for cross-line patterns","tool_groups.item_13":"Keep a sandbox instance alive in the background after the task ends (instances are otherwise auto-reclaimed). Provide the instance id and a reason. Fails when the background sandbox limit (5) is already reached, returning the currently kept sandboxes (id, reason, how long they have existed) so you can drop one with SandboxDrop and retry.","tool_groups.item_14":"Retrieves output from a running or completed background task.\\\\n- Takes a task_id parameter identifying the task\\\\n- Use block=true (default) to wait until the task completes or timeout ms elapses (whichever comes first); default timeout is 30000 ms, max 600000 ms\\\\n- Use block=false for a non-blocking single-snapshot status check\\\\n- Works with both explicit Bash(run_in_background=true) task ids and the backgroundTaskId returned when a foreground command was auto-moved to the background (long-running commands beyond the assistant\'\'s blocking budget)","tool_groups.item_15":"Stops a running background task by its ID.\\n- Returns a success or failure status\\n- Use when you need to terminate a long-running background task","tool_groups.item_17":"Writes a file to the local filesystem.\\\\n\\\\nUsage:\\\\n- This tool will overwrite the existing file if there is one at the provided path.\\\\n- If this is an existing file, you MUST use the Read tool first.\\\\n- Prefer the Edit tool for modifying existing files \u2014 it only sends the diff.\\\\n- Only use this tool to create new files or for complete rewrites.","tool_groups.item_18":"Finish the Local File task and provide the final answer.","tool_groups.item_19":"Ask the user a free-form question. The user reply (or skip) is surfaced via the QuickReplyBlock scene:2 UI.","tool_groups.item_2":"Batch-upload one or more local sandbox files to the public network and return file tokens. Input: path_list (array of absolute paths, max 50).","tool_groups.item_20":"Ask the user to authorize additional Local File folder access.","tool_groups.item_21":"Ask the user to perform a manual action (browser takeover, Windows PIP operation, file upload, or PIP environment initialization). Required params: \\"type\\" and \\"display_message\\". Use {computerEnvInitUserRequestActionTypePrompt} when computer_env_init reports the PIP system environment needs initialization; use {pipUserRequestActionTypePrompt} for Windows PIP login or desktop-operation help; use type=browserControl only for browser takeover; type=fileUpload for file upload. \\"display_message\\" is the user-facing guide text \u2014 it renders as the on-screen guidance and the desktop notification body. Surfaces a QuickReplyBlock scene:2 with action items.","tool_groups.item_22":"Ask the user to confirm a high-risk Local File operation.","tool_groups.item_6":"Replaces the contents of a specific cell in a Jupyter notebook (.ipynb file). The notebook_path must be an absolute path. Cells are identified by cell_id (not cell_number). Use edit_mode=insert to add a new cell (inserted after cell_id, or appended to the end of the notebook if cell_id is omitted). Use edit_mode=delete to remove a cell.","tool_groups.item_8":"Drop (destroy and reclaim) a background sandbox instance by its instance id. Use this to free a slot when the background sandbox limit is reached.","tool_groups.read":"Reads a file from the local filesystem.\\\\n\\\\nUsage:\\\\n- The file_path parameter must be an absolute path, not a relative path\\\\n- By default, it reads the whole file; for large files use offset/limit to paginate\\\\n- offset and limit are TOKEN-based (unit: estimated tokens via bbpe155k tokenizer; English 1 token \u2248 4 chars, Chinese 1 token \u2248 1.7 chars). Do NOT confuse with line count. Passing limit=20 returns ~20 tokens (a few words), NOT 20 lines. Default limit is 4000 tokens; omit limit entirely unless you need pagination.\\\\n- This tool can read Jupyter notebooks (.ipynb files) and images\\\\n- For PDF files, use the pages parameter to specify a page range (e.g. \\"1-5\\")\\\\n- This tool can only read files, not directories. To read a directory, use ls via Bash.\\\\n- You can call multiple Read tools in a single response for batch reading.","tool_groups.